<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abul Hasan Qutb Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1699">1699</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Tana Shah, Abul Hasan Tana Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abul Hasan Qutb Shah, known as Tana Shah, was the last sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, reigning from 1672 to 1687. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> ministers Madanna and Akkanna ran the government. The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> besieged Golconda for eight months and took it by treachery in 1687, imprisoning him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>, where he died in 1699.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abul-hasan-qutb-shah.jpg" alt="Abul Hasan Qutb Shah" width="300" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: thesandiegomuseumofartcollection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sultan_Abu%27l_Hasan_of_Golconda,_Standing_(6124509941).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In his early life Abul Hasan had no connection with the royal court. Muslim historians describe him as a distant descendant of the Qutb Shahi family in the male line, lazy and given to drink, who for a time followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> preacher Sayyad Kathal; he has been traced as a grandson of one of Sultan Muhammad Quli's nephews. He married the third daughter of Sultan Abdullah Qutb Shah, who had no male heir. When Abdullah died, the choice lay between his sons-in-law; with one in prison, the Muslim generals backed Abul Hasan against Sayyad Ahmad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The eighth and last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahi dynasty</a>, he became known as Tana Shah, King of Taste, for his love of the arts and pleasure. Under the early sultans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> had been forbidden to observe their festivals, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</a> (1580–1611) let them celebrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a> and Holi openly. Abul Hasan extended that policy by placing Madanna and Akkanna in charge of tax collection and the exchequer. Their reforms made them very powerful and set off factional strife with the Muslim elite, who turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>; one of the sultan's generals defected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. A Mughal force led by Aurangzeb's son beheaded the two ministers, plundered the sultanate and killed many Hindus in its administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died after twelve years of captivity in Daulatabad Fort, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a>, and was buried in a modest grave at Khuldabad, in the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Shah Raju Qattal, whose descendant he revered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> became part of the six Mughal provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and Mahabat Khan, once commander of the Qutb Shahi army, became its governor. His only son, Khuda Banda, born during his captivity, was taken to an unknown place after his father's death.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> tradition, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mentor called him Tana Shah as a term of affection, meaning a child saint or pious king. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi, however, tānāshāh came to mean an autocratic or despotic ruler, and the name passed into idiom across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> as a byword for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abul Hasan Qutb Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aurangzeb besieged Golconda for eight months and took it by treachery in 1687, imprisoning the last sultan, <b>Abul Hasan Tana Shah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Daulatabad, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.36,78.48&amp;year=1689" aria-label="Hyderabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6682 1519 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abul Hasan Qutb Shah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7132 1789h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7132 1789h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.36,78.48&amp;year=1689">What was here in 1689? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
